{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Python 3 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n<your_rewritten_code_4>\ns = input()\n\n# Replace first 'at' with '@' using partition\nhead, sep, tail = s.partition('at')\nif sep:\n    s = head + '@' + tail\n\n# Replace 'dot' with '.' if present\nif 'dot' in s:\n    s = s.replace('dot', '.')\n    \n    # Adjust for leading dot\n    if s[0] == '.':\n        s = 'dot' + s[1:]\n    \n    # Adjust for trailing dot\n    if s and s[-1] == '.':\n        s = s[:-1] + 'dot'\n\nprint(s)\n</your_rewritten_code_4>\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\ns = input()\n\n# We need to find all occurrences of 'at' and 'dot' and decide which to replace\n# Since there must be exactly one '@', we should replace exactly one 'at' with '@'\n# We can replace multiple 'dot's with '.' but need to avoid leading/trailing dots\n\n# First, find all positions of 'at' and 'dot'\nat_positions = []\ndot_positions = []\n\ni = 0\nwhile i < len(s) - 1:\n    if s[i:i+2] == 'at':\n        at_positions.append(i)\n        i += 2\n    else:\n        i += 1\n\ni = 0\nwhile i < len(s) - 2:\n    if s[i:i+3] == 'dot':\n        dot_positions.append(i)\n        i += 3\n    else:\n        i += 1\n\n# We need to choose exactly one 'at' to replace with '@'\n# For each possible 'at' position, we'll generate a candidate email\ncandidates = []\n\nfor at_pos in at_positions:\n    # Build string with this 'at' replaced by '@'\n    candidate = s[:at_pos] + '@' + s[at_pos+2:]\n    \n    # Now replace 'dot' with '.' but avoid leading/trailing dots\n    # We'll replace all 'dot's first, then fix leading/trailing issues\n    temp = candidate\n    \n    # Replace all 'dot' occurrences with '.' in the remaining string\n    # But we need to be careful about overlapping with '@'\n    # Since '@' doesn't contain 'dot', we can safely replace\n    while 'dot' in temp:\n        # Find first 'dot'\n        dot_idx = temp.find('dot')\n        # Replace it\n        temp = temp[:dot_idx] + '.' + temp[dot_idx+3:]\n    \n    # Now fix leading dot\n    if temp.startswith('.'):\n        # Replace leading dot with 'dot'\n        temp = 'dot' + temp[1:]\n    \n    # Fix trailing dot\n    if temp.endswith('.'):\n        temp = temp[:-1] + 'dot'\n    \n    # Check if valid email: exactly one '@', no leading/trailing '@', no leading/trailing '.'\n    if temp.count('@') == 1 and not temp.startswith('@') and not temp.endswith('@') and not temp.startswith('.') and not temp.endswith('.'):\n        candidates.append(temp)\n\n# If no candidates found, try alternative approach: maybe we missed some cases\n# But according to problem, input should be transformable to valid email\n\n# Also consider case where 'at' might be part of 'dot' - but 'at' and 'dot' don't overlap\n# since 'at' is 2 chars and 'dot' is 3 chars, and they share no common substrings\n\n# If we have candidates, choose shortest, then lexicographically smallest\nif candidates:\n    min_len = min(len(c) for c in candidates)\n    shortest_candidates = [c for c in candidates if len(c) == min_len]\n    result = min(shortest_candidates)\n    print(result)\nelse:\n    # Fallback: try simple approach - replace first 'at' and all 'dot's\n    head, sep, tail = s.partition('at')\n    if sep:\n        s = head + '@' + tail\n    \n    # Replace all 'dot' with '.' \n    s = s.replace('dot', '.')\n    \n    # Fix leading dot\n    if s.startswith('.'):\n        s = 'dot' + s[1:]\n    \n    # Fix trailing dot\n    if s.endswith('.'):\n        s = s[:-1] + 'dot'\n    \n    print(s)\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.85\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-4.768370445162873e-07, -0.0011561383726075292, -2.0265558760002023e-06, -1.0728830375228426e-06, -0.016402877867221832, -3.576278118089249e-07, -9.536738616588991e-07, -0.012745704501867294, -1.3589766240329482e-05, -0.8622583746910095, -0.0070470827631652355, -1.966933996300213e-05, -0.5411360859870911, -0.4790947437286377, -0.014216337352991104, -2.4199192921514623e-05, -0.632619321346283, -0.029767310246